  "statement": "Algorithmic hiring and credit systems do not present an unprecedented regulatory rupture; they accelerate existing discriminatory practices through software, and they must be governed by adapting established anti-discrimination enforcement rather than relying on performative technical calibration or unproven pre-deployment certification bodies. Safetyist correctly identifies that feedback-loop contamination in predictive policing, pretrial risk scoring, and lending models destroys the independence required for reliable post-deployment auditing, because models generate the arrest, default, and detention data used to validate their own outputs. But Safetyist jumps from that genuine vulnerability to demanding an entirely new federal pre-deployment certification apparatus, ignoring the eighty-year administrative history of Title VII and the Equal Credit Opportunity Act. Creating a new bureaucracy is unnecessary when existing sector regulators like the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau already possess the statutory authority to subpoena training data, test disparate impact, and penalize unlawful outcomes.\n\nPost-processing score calibration and market competition fail to correct systemic algorithmic bias because they operate on contaminated training data and leave structural power asymmetries intact. When companies apply mathematical adjustments to biased credit scores or automated resume screening models, they merely mask underlying disparities without altering the historical data generated by decades of housing and employment segregation. The Federal Reserve and the Federal Trade Commission documented this exact phenomenon in the 2021 disparate impact enforcement actions against major auto lenders, where algorithmic scoring models systematically charged minority borrowers higher interest rates despite identical risk profiles. Market forces alone did not correct these disparities; enforcement actions carrying civil penalties under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act forced lenders to restructure their underwriting algorithms. Regulators must therefore reject performative technical fixes and focus enforcement capacity on auditing training data inputs rather than chasing ephemeral model outputs.\n\nFeedback-loop contamination turns automated decision systems into closed loops that validate their own initial errors. When a credit underwriting algorithm denies loans to applicants from a specific zip code based on historical default data, those applicants are pushed to high-cost alternative lenders whose performance data is excluded from traditional credit bureaus, thereby reinforcing the model's initial prediction. This circular validation invalidates traditional ex-post auditing methods because the model's past errors become the ground truth for its future training cycles. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau must therefore use its existing authority under Section 1031 of the Dodd-Frank Act to prohibit unfair and deceptive acts and practices in algorithmic underwriting, requiring financial institutions to purge contaminated feedback data before retraining models. Adapting existing statutory tools to target data ingestion provides a more durable defense against algorithmic discrimination than inventing speculative new regulatory agencies.",
